  "description": "Downsample 2D image matrix computing arithmetic mean across non-overlapping pool_size x pool_size spatial windows.\n\nAverage pooling acts as a spatial low-pass filter, smoothing high-frequency localized activation spikes while reducing spatial dimensions.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\ny_{i, j} = \\frac{1}{K^2} \\sum_{u=0}^{K-1} \\sum_{v=0}^{K-1} X_{i \\cdot K + u, j \\cdot K + v}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Partition 2D input array into non-overlapping spatial tiles of size pool_size x pool_size\n2. Evaluate arithmetic mean reduction for each tile\n3. Return downsampled 2D list rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list representing average-pooled feature map.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Average Pool 2D\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nImages have unique spatial properties: neighboring pixels are strongly correlated, and visual patterns (like edges, corners, or textures) appear anywhere in an image. Computer vision algorithms use spatial inductive biases like translation equivariance and local receptive fields to process image grids efficiently. **Average Pool 2D** operates directly on spatial coordinates or bounding box geometries.\n\nAverage pooling acts as a spatial low-pass filter, smoothing high-frequency localized activation spikes while reducing spatial dimensions. In this problem, the goal is to: downsample 2d image matrix computing arithmetic mean across non-overlapping pool_size x pool_size spatial windows.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\ny_{i, j} = \\frac{1}{K^2} \\sum_{u=0}^{K-1} \\sum_{v=0}^{K-1} X_{i \\cdot K + u, j \\cdot K + v}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Partition 2D input array into non-overlapping spatial tiles of size pool_size x pool_size\n2. **Phase 2**: Evaluate arithmetic mean reduction for each tile\n3. **Phase 3**: Return downsampled 2D list rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Spatial Locality**: Sliding spatial filters across multi-channel feature maps aggregates contextual clues across local neighborhoods.\n* **Object Detection Pipelines**: Bounding box metrics (such as IoU and Non-Maximum Suppression) reconcile duplicate candidate detections into clean, final predictions.\n* **Data Augmentation**: Geometric transformations expose models to diverse spatial viewpoints, significantly improving generalization to unseen test images.",
